  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A bird's foot sat in a cave for 3,300 years. Cavers found it on Mount Owen, near Nelson, in January 1987.",
        "visual_query": "new zealand limestone cave passage",
        "visual_fallback": "limestone cave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was not a fossil. Muscle and sinew were still attached to the bone, dried hard and unmistakably there.",
        "visual_query": "museum specimen bird bone closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "bone specimen"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The foot belonged to an upland moa, a flightless bird under a metre tall. So why did the cave keep it and not the rest?",
        "visual_query": "new zealand south island forest",
        "visual_fallback": "native forest"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Dry, cold cave air stops decay almost completely. Outside, every other upland moa was gone within a century of people reaching New Zealand.",
        "visual_query": "cold dark cave interior stalactites",
        "visual_fallback": "dark cave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So the foot is older than the extinction that erased its species. It now sits in Te Papa, in Wellington, catalogued as specimen S.023808.",
        "visual_query": "wellington museum natural history gallery",
        "visual_fallback": "museum gallery"
      }
    ],
